Cell["\<\
KCBS takes its name from Klyachko, Can, Binicioglu and Shumovsky (2008), who \
derived what they called the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]pentagram inequality\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] for a three-dimensional quantum system (a qutrit): \
five unit vectors on a cone, each orthogonal to its two cyclic neighbors, \
giving five dichotomic observables A_i = 1 - 2|v_i\[RightAngleBracket]\
\[LeftAngleBracket]v_i| with eigenvalues \[PlusMinus]1. Because neighboring \
directions are orthogonal, the pair (A_i, A_i+1) is jointly measurable \
\[LongDash] exactly the five edges of the C5 exclusivity graph above. Any \
noncontextual hidden-variable assignment obeys the cyclic sum \[CapitalSigma] \
\[LeftAngleBracket]A_i A_i+1\[RightAngleBracket] \[GreaterEqual] -3; the \
qutrit instead reaches \[CapitalSigma] = 5 - 4\[Sqrt]5 \[TildeTilde] -3.944 \
at the cone-axis state, where each projector has quantum probability \
1/\[Sqrt]5.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0a370bb3-7599-a14b-9c7b-26538e9b7826"],

Cell["\<\
Verify the cone-angle geometry live: the per-event quantum probability, and \
that summing it over all five pentagon events reproduces \[CurlyTheta](C5):\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"d21b02c5-cf68-9c4a-9e42-8c7c90b2126c"],

Cell["\<\
Simplify[{Cos[Pi/5]/(1 + Cos[Pi/5]), 5*Cos[Pi/5]/(1 + Cos[Pi/5])}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"1a374cc0-b825-0c42-91dd-cf666024adc2"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   FractionBox["1", 
    SqrtBox["5"]], ",", 
   SqrtBox["5"]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"b88a96f2-6b32-4445-83bd-\
e0bf43974c26"],

Cell["\<\
The geometry deserves to be seen, and its numbers deserve operational \
meaning. Each direction v_k is a detector; on the handle state (the cone's \
axis) it clicks with probability |\[LeftAngleBracket]handle|v_k\
\[RightAngleBracket]|^2, and since all five rays lean on the axis at the same \
angle, all five click probabilities are equal \[LongDash] the blue curve. \
Exclusivity is the second, sharper requirement: adjacent directions must be \
perpendicular, so that their projectors commute and can be tested in a single \
run \[LongDash] and if v_k has just fired, the probability that its neighbor \
v_(k+1) also fires is the squared overlap |\[LeftAngleBracket]v_k|v_(k+1)\
\[RightAngleBracket]|^2, which must be EXACTLY zero for the two events to be \
mutually exclusive. That crosstalk between supposedly-exclusive detectors is \
the coral curve, and it is the quantity that makes the cone angle rigid: the \
two curves meet the KCBS specification at exactly one half-angle, \[Theta]* = \
ArcCos[5^(-1/4)] \[TildeTilde] 48.06\[Degree], where each detector clicks \
with probability 1/\[Sqrt]5 \[TildeTilde] 0.447 and the crosstalk is exactly \
zero. Five degrees of detuning already lets \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]exclusive\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] detectors co-fire 2.5% of the time. Connecting \
consecutive tips traces the pentagram that gave KCBS's inequality its \
original name; every marked value below is measured through \
QuantumMeasurementOperator, not read off the drawing:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"cc63dd84-438f-f642-8bcf-fb2cf38f9371"],

Cell["\<\
Draw the five directions on their cone, and track both operational \
probabilities \[LongDash] per-event click rate on the handle state, and \
neighbor crosstalk after a click \[LongDash] across all cone angles, marking \
the QuantumMeasurementOperator-computed values at and near \[Theta]*:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"6216c86d-b585-a646-a4c2-556ae920176c"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{c2, th, vv, vecs, projsAt, handle, qClick, qCross, qCrossOff, tstar, \
blue, coral, tealc, ink, scene, curves}, blue = RGBColor[0.2, 0.45, 0.85]; \
coral = RGBColor[0.6, 0.24, 0.11]; tealc = RGBColor[0.06, 0.43, 0.34]; ink = \
GrayLevel[0.15]; c2 = Cos[Pi/5]/(1 + Cos[Pi/5]); th = ArcCos[Sqrt[c2]]; tstar \
= N[th/Degree]; vv[t_] := Table[N[{Sin[t]*Cos[4*Pi*(j/5)], \
Sin[t]*Sin[4*Pi*(j/5)], Cos[t]}, 20], {j, 0, 4}]; vecs = vv[th]; projsAt[t_] \
:= (Outer[Times, #1, #1] & ) /@ vv[t]; handle = QuantumState[{0, 0, 1}, 3]; \
qClick = QuantumMeasurementOperator[projsAt[th][[1]]][handle][\"Mean\"]; \
qCross = QuantumMeasurementOperator[projsAt[th][[2]]][QuantumState[vecs[[1]], \
3]][\"Mean\"]; qCrossOff = QuantumMeasurementOperator[projsAt[th - \
5*Degree][[2]]][QuantumState[vv[th - 5*Degree][[1]], 3]][\"Mean\"]; scene = \
Graphics3D[{{Opacity[0.12], tealc, Cone[{{0, 0, 1.02}, {0, 0, 0}}, \
1.02*Tan[th]]}, {coral, Thickness[0.006], Arrowheads[0.03], Arrow[{{0, 0, 0}, \
{0, 0, 1.24}}]}, Text[Style[\"axis = handle state\", 10, coral], {0, 0, \
1.56}], Table[{blue, Thickness[0.005], Arrowheads[0.025], Arrow[{{0, 0, 0}, \
