Cell["\<\
Both single-qutrit gates, and the assembled three-gate circuit, are confirmed \
unitary by the framework itself \[LongDash] not merely asserted \[LongDash] \
and the circuit's own matrix has determinant exactly -1: two rotations \
compose to a rotation, and the one reflection flips the overall parity. This \
is the same fact, now checked as an actual gate sequence rather than argued \
combinatorially, that makes a single twisted joint irreducible while two \
consecutive ones partially cancel \[LongDash] the reason ddt (isolated flips) \
beats pure twisted (paired flips) later in this essay:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"4ceeb71a-58e1-c842-88de-8b6033c3a2a7"],

Cell["\<\
{QuantumOperator[Rdirect, 3][\"UnitaryQ\"], QuantumOperator[Rtwisted, \
3][\"UnitaryQ\"], Det[QuantumCircuitOperator[{QuantumOperator[Rdirect, 3], \
QuantumOperator[Rdirect, 3], QuantumOperator[Rtwisted, 3]}][\"Matrix\"]]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"6f84ba94-cb3f-f34b-8ea8-e87fd9855581"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"True", ",", "True", ",", 
   RowBox[{"-", "1.`14.469947092258117"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->\
"1df32fed-eadf-9e42-96d7-cf33f26c90de"],

Cell["\<\
Why exactly two ways to attach at a joint \[LongDash] not more, not fewer? \
Because the number of surviving attachments is set by the size of the \
interface: the part of the next pentagon's frame that the joint pins down. A \
rigid attachment is an isometry of the same real three-dimensional space the \
qutrit lives in, and it must match the pin. Pin one shared edge \[LongDash] \
two orthonormal directions \[LongDash] and the isometry is already determined \
on the plane they span, so the only freedom left is the sign on the \
one-dimensional orthogonal complement: exactly two attachments, separated by \
exactly det = \[PlusMinus]1. Direct and twisted are not two choices among \
many \[LongDash] they are the entire solution set, and R_twisted = \
diag(1,1,-1).R_direct displays the leftover sign flip literally. Pin only a \
shared vertex \[LongDash] one direction \[LongDash] and a whole circle of \
rotations about it survives: a continuum of genuinely different attachments \
that the determinant no longer separates (every one has det = +1), which is \
why a vertex-glued mesh admits no two-letter word description. Pin two shared \
edges \[LongDash] three independent directions \[LongDash] and nothing \
survives: the map is forced outright, sign included, and the \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]new\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] pentagon collapses onto \
the old one. Edge-gluing is the unique interface size at which composition \
becomes a discrete binary choice \[LongDash] the reason an infinite gluing \
strategy is exactly a word in {d,t}, and the reason the de Bruijn machinery \
of the closing sections applies at all. All three regimes are counted below, \
exactly:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"abb6b8ce-34d5-604d-b944-afe2b0fba275"],

Cell["\<\
Pin one edge, one vertex, then two edges of the pentagon frame, and solve for \
every isometry matching the pin \[LongDash] two attachments (reconstructing \
R_direct and R_twisted exactly), a continuum (held for a symbolic spin angle \
phi), and a single forced map, respectively:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"c89de452-9810-8143-9421-5ab019f83dc7"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{c2, vv, attach, edgePair, spin, phi, samples, Vmat, Wmat, Rforced, \
Rd, Rt}, c2 = Cos[Pi/5]/(1 + Cos[Pi/5]); vv[k_] := {Sqrt[c2], Sqrt[1 - \
c2]*Cos[4*Pi*(k/5)], Sqrt[1 - c2]*Sin[4*Pi*(k/5)]}; Rd = \
RotationMatrix[4*(Pi/5), {1, 0, 0}]; Rt = DiagonalMatrix[{1, 1, -1}] . Rd; \
attach[{a1_, a2_}, {b1_, b2_}, s_] := Transpose[{b1, b2, s*Cross[b1, b2]}] . \
{a1, a2, Cross[a1, a2]}; edgePair = {attach[{vv[0], vv[1]}, {vv[1], vv[2]}, \
1], attach[{vv[0], vv[1]}, {vv[1], vv[2]}, -1]}; spin[a_] := \
RotationMatrix[a, vv[0]]; samples = Table[spin[a] . vv[1], {a, 2*(Pi/5), \
2*Pi, 2*(Pi/5)}]; Vmat = Transpose[{vv[0], vv[1], vv[2]}]; Wmat = \
Transpose[{vv[1], vv[2], vv[3]}]; Rforced = Wmat . Inverse[Vmat]; \
Association[\"one edge pinned: dets of the two surviving attachments\" -> \
FullSimplify[Det /@ edgePair], \"  the det +1 one is R_direct, exactly\" -> \
FullSimplify[edgePair[[1]] == Rd], \"  the det -1 one for the mirrored target \
is R_twisted, exactly\" -> FullSimplify[attach[{vv[0], vv[1]}, {vv[4], \
vv[3]}, -1] == Rt], \"one vertex pinned: interface held at every spin angle \
phi, always det +1\" -> FullSimplify[spin[phi] . vv[0] == vv[0] && \
Det[spin[phi]] == 1], \"  distinct attachments among five sample spin \
angles\" -> Length[DeleteDuplicates[Round[N[samples], 10^(-10)]]], \"two \
edges pinned: forced map is orthogonal, is R_direct, sign no longer free\" -> \
FullSimplify[{Rforced . Transpose[Rforced] == IdentityMatrix[3], Rforced == \
Rd, Det[Rforced]}]]]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"9db93fec-7d02-004f-b959-eee830238180"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"\<\"one edge pinned: dets of the two surviving attachments\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"1", ",", 
      RowBox[{"-", "1"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"  the det +1 one is R_direct, exactly\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"  the det -1 one for the mirrored target is R_twisted, \
exactly\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"one vertex pinned: interface held at every spin angle phi, \
always det +1\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"  distinct attachments among five sample spin angles\"\>", "\[Rule]",
     "5"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"two edges pinned: forced map is orthogonal, is R_direct, sign \
no longer free\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"True", ",", "True", ",", "1"}], "}"}]}]}], 
  "\[RightAssociation]"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"395c0e56-d332-f948-b364-\
0244f9a17f33"],

Cell["\<\
For a ring built entirely from one orientation, both closed forms below are \
proven theorems, not numerical fits \[LongDash] derived here, not merely \
cited. Start with the direct ring's \[CurlyTheta]: deleting the N glue edges \
leaves a disjoint union C_N \[SquareUnion] C_2N (the inner rail plus the \
outer cycle); \[CurlyTheta] never decreases under edge deletion, is additive \
on disjoint unions, and \[CurlyTheta](C_2N) = N since even cycles are perfect \
\[LongDash] giving the upper bound \[CurlyTheta](direct-ring N) \[LessEqual] \
N + \[CurlyTheta](C_N). A matching lower bound comes from an explicit vector \
representation: take an optimal representation of the rail C_N, append one \
dimension, and assign every outer vertex the same fresh basis vector \
\[LongDash] every edge then either repeats a rail orthogonality or pairs with \
the new dimension, so the construction is feasible and attains exactly N + \
\[CurlyTheta](C_N).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9fcc8fc5-f73d-2d4a-8d28-815ff78131f6"],

Cell["\<\
Spot-check the representation witness at four odd N \[LongDash] cyclic \
orthogonality (including wrap-around), unit norm, and the value identity all \
verified live:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"76c5030c-84d1-0344-b5ec-0158fd59837f"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{w = Pi*((n - 1)/n), ca2, u, handle}, ca2 = Cos[Pi/n]/(1 + Cos[Pi/n]); \
u[k_] := {Sqrt[ca2], Sqrt[1 - ca2]*Cos[k*w], Sqrt[1 - ca2]*Sin[k*w], 0}; \
handle = {1, 0, 0, 0}; AllTrue[Range[0, n - 1], FullSimplify[u[#1] . u[Mod[#1 \
+ 1, n]]] === 0 & ] && AllTrue[Range[0, n - 1], FullSimplify[u[#1] . u[#1]] \
=== 1 & ] && FullSimplify[Sum[(handle . u[k])^2, {k, 0, n - 1}] + n - (n + \
n*(Cos[Pi/n]/(1 + Cos[Pi/n])))] === 0]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b6eaee21-075f-9f4c-b8a0-8262fcd215ed"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"True", ",", "True", ",", "True", ",", "True"}], "}"}]], "Output",Ex\
pressionUUID->"ecb5faad-be50-e148-94ff-f1f932851e45"],

Cell["\<\
The matching \[Alpha] bound comes from counting, not geometry: every pentagon \
induces its own C5 (independence number 2), and summing this window bound \
over all N pentagons counts each glue's shared vertices twice and its free \
vertex once, giving 2\[CenterDot](shared) + (free) \[LessEqual] 2N and hence \
\[Alpha] \[LessEqual] \[LeftFloor]3N/2\[RightFloor]; the alternating set of \
every free vertex plus every other rail vertex attains it. For even N this \
already forces \[CurlyTheta] = \[Alpha] = \[Alpha]* = 3N/2 exactly \
\[LongDash] no possible gap, at any level of the hierarchy.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f487fde7-544f-d746-913f-eca63d41b2d1"],

Cell["\<\
At odd N the gap does not vanish \[LongDash] but it does not grow either. \
Using \[CurlyTheta](C_N) = N Cos[\[Pi]/N]/(1+Cos[\[Pi]/N]) for odd N together \
with \[Alpha] = (3N-1)/2, the gap works out to \[CurlyTheta](C_N) - (N-1)/2; \
expand it as N \[Rule] \[Infinity]:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"58f61dda-1ee1-e749-bd46-25303649776d"],

Cell["\<\
Series[n*Cos[Pi/n]/(1 + Cos[Pi/n]) - (n - 1)/2, {n, Infinity, 2}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"5298cc55-de48-3649-9bc4-0f44a5f07b25"],

Cell[BoxData[
 InterpretationBox[
  RowBox[{
   FractionBox["1", "2"], "-", 
   FractionBox[
    SuperscriptBox["\[Pi]", "2"], 
    RowBox[{"8", " ", "n"}]], "+", 
   InterpretationBox[
    SuperscriptBox[
     RowBox[{"O", "[", 
      FractionBox["1", "n"], "]"}], "3"],
    SeriesData[$CellContext`n, 
     DirectedInfinity[1], {}, 0, 3, 1],
    Editable->False]}],
  SeriesData[$CellContext`n, 
   DirectedInfinity[1], {
    Rational[1, 2], Rational[-1, 8] Pi^2}, 0, 3, 1],
  Editable->False]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"6ad53b37-a09e-494b-9bf5-\
26398ff1dc9b"],

Cell["\<\
So odd-N direct rings approach a fixed ceiling of 1/2 as N grows, closing in \
at rate \[Pi]^2/(8N) \[LongDash] compare the series' N -> 17 prediction \
against the 0.4270 already plotted in Figure 4 below. Direct is bounded \
regardless of size; twisted, next, is not.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"28f815db-c4bb-5b40-84ba-9aa35638138f"],

Cell["\<\
The twisted ring's \[CurlyTheta] \[TildeTilde] \[Tau]*\[CenterDot]N is \
likewise not a fit: \[Tau]* is an exact algebraic closed form. As N \[Rule] \
\[Infinity] the ring's \[CurlyTheta]-SDP reduces to a continuum symbol \
minimax; the ring's reflection symmetry forces one dual variable to equal \
another (\[Beta]bx = \[Gamma]ax), and the KKT stationarity condition factors \
as (u-2g)(u+2gc) = 0, forcing a second relation (\[Beta]ab = 2\[Gamma]ba). \
Substituting both into the remaining system and eliminating variables by Gr\
\[ODoubleDot]bner-basis computation collapses it to a single cubic, 49x^3 - \
128x^2 - 75x + 218 = 0, and \[Tau]* is its middle root, \[TildeTilde] \
1.376718.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"25b11941-c41d-c447-ae1b-56e25379b313"],

Cell["\<\
Certify \[Tau]* as a genuine solution: exhibit the explicit dual witness (g, \
h, cos \[Theta]*) in the cubic field \[DoubleStruckCapitalQ](\[Tau]*) and \
verify all three KKT/feasibility identities vanish exactly:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"66c5fccd-937f-9048-8ee6-4e21eb259c67"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{gW, hW, cW}, {gW, hW, cW} = {(53*tauStar^2 - 121*tauStar + 218)/458, \
(327 - 67*tauStar - 35*tauStar^2)/229, (1715*tauStar^2 + 77*tauStar - \
3428)/916}; RootReduce[Flatten[{gW*tauStar - hW^2*(1 + 2*cW), tauStar^2 - (3 \
- 3*gW)*tauStar + (2 - 3*gW) - 2*(1 - hW)^2, CoefficientList[tauStar^3 - \
(5*gW^2 + 4*gW^2*\[FormalC] + 2*hW^2)*tauStar + 2*hW^2*gW*(2*\[FormalC] + 2*\
\[FormalC]^2 - 1) - 4*gW*hW^2*(\[FormalC] - cW)^2, \[FormalC]]}]]]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"91728913-e393-9c48-8d7e-c8ecbe6f15d9"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"0", ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", "0"}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID\
->"0415c4e5-812a-114c-8b87-05d4533c5a58"],

Cell["\<\
Global optimality \[LongDash] not just a stationary point \[LongDash] follows \
because the symbol program is a max-eigenvalue minimax, hence convex, and \
this witness has both KKT multipliers positive. With both orientations now \
proven, not asserted: a direct ring of N pentagons has \[CurlyTheta] = N + \
\[CurlyTheta](C_N) and \[Alpha] = \[LeftFloor]3N/2\[RightFloor] (gap = 0 at \
even N, \[Rule] 1/2 at odd N); a twisted ring has \[CurlyTheta] \[TildeTilde] \
\[Tau]*\[CenterDot]N and \[Alpha] = \[LeftFloor]4N/3\[RightFloor] (gap = (\
\[Tau]*-4/3)N, unbounded). Same pentagons, same count \[LongDash] one \
orientation saturates at a ceiling, the other grows without bound:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"bd30a84e-291f-b048-8610-ee04269f28ee"],

Cell["\<\
The contrast plotted \[LongDash] bounded versus linear, not just \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]small versus big\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"20ab43a9-ec08-ef41-bed0-a65e31556b50"],

Cell["\<\
Plot the cited quantum advantage (\[CurlyTheta] - \[Alpha]) against ring size \
N for both orientations:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"6a133f9d-f126-5749-ab63-91305fdb4d7e"],

Cell["\<\
ListLinePlot[{Transpose[{Range[3, 17], {0., 0.5014, 0.7702, 0.0979, 0.6037, \
1.0028, 0.3097, 0.6792, 1.1336, 0.5041, 0.8336, 1.2191, 0.6484, 1.0058, \
1.3519}}], Transpose[{Range[3, 17], {0., 0., 0.2361, 0., 0.3177, 0., 0.3601, \
0., 0.3863, 0., 0.4042, 0., 0.4171, 0., 0.427}}]}, PlotMarkers -> Automatic, \
PlotLegends -> {\"twisted-ring\", \"direct-ring\"}, AxesLabel -> {\"N \
(pentagons in the ring)\", \"quantum advantage \[CurlyTheta]-\[Alpha]\"}, \
PlotRange -> {{2.5, 17.5}, {-0.1, 1.5}}, ImageSize -> 480, ImagePadding -> \
{{45, 15}, {40, 28}}, PlotLabel -> \"twisted grows with size (extensive); \
direct stays bounded \[LongDash] orientation, not count, decides\"]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"36252fae-6f59-904c-a20e-64f2fbd76ca8"],

