Cell["\<\
But the bound never reaches gap(ddt) exactly, and it provably cannot be \
forced to by this method alone: this class of ergodic-optimization problems \
is known to fail the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]finiteness property\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] in general (Bousch\[Dash]Mairesse, 2002) \[LongDash] \
the true optimal word can, in principle, be a non-periodic (Sturmian) \
sequence that no search over periodic words, however long, will ever \
encounter. So no amount of extending this series can, by itself, prove that \
ddt is the global optimum.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1602d39e-d123-5f49-92fc-341f4de41777"],

Cell["From Gluing Words to Black-Box Certification", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"86d17985-334c-e241-986e-7be9a42b91d4"],

Cell["\<\
Everything so far is composition mathematics; this closing section is what it \
is for \[LongDash] and why the essay carries the title it does. Return to the \
sealed box that opened the first section: a device claims to be quantum, and \
only its click statistics can be observed. The pentagon is the smallest \
interrogation protocol whose quantum answer no classical hidden-variable \
account can match, and the gap \[CurlyTheta] - \[Alpha] is the margin of that \
impossibility \[LongDash] the certifiable advantage per interrogation round. \
A mesh is therefore a certification instrument, and its gluing word is a \
design parameter: it sets how the margin scales with instrument size. Direct \
rings collapse the margin outright (\[CurlyTheta] = \[Alpha] at even N: a \
larger instrument with zero statistical power); twisted rings hold a fixed \
margin of \[Tau]* - 4/3 \[TildeTilde] 0.0434 per pentagon; the ddt word \
raises it to \[TildeTilde] 0.0699 \[LongDash] the 61% of the abstract. \
Optimizing gap density, the business of the preceding sections, is optimizing \
the statistical power of a black-box test per unit of hardware.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"56fe4d3a-9966-cf46-b774-bef228448ff0"],

Cell["\<\
The quantum side of such a test stays remarkably cheap. The twisted-chain \
bound is realized, at every length, by the same three-level system Lapkiewicz \
et al. (2011) drove through one pentagon \[LongDash] a single photon on three \
optical paths; growing the mesh grows only the list of measurement settings, \
never the dimension. The noise requirement does not run away with size \
either: under isotropic noise the critical visibility \[LongDash] the signal \
fraction below which the quantum advantage disappears \[LongDash] rises from \
0.5854 for a single pentagon but saturates at 1/(3(\[Tau]* - 1)) \
\[TildeTilde] 0.8848 in the long-chain limit. A fixed ~88.5% visibility \
target, independent of instrument size, is an unusually forgiving scaling law \
for an entanglement-free contextuality experiment.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"06d88396-4a5a-0d44-9ce1-17157603dc5a"],

Cell["\<\
Both endpoints of that visibility law, exactly \[LongDash] the \
single-pentagon value and the long-chain ceiling, from the same algebraic \
\[Tau]*:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"a979726e-8644-1d42-97bf-e4e902b3192b"],

Cell["\<\
tauStar = Root[49 #^3 - 128 #^2 - 75 # + 218 &, 2];
{FullSimplify[(2 - 5/3)/(Sqrt[5] - 5/3)], N[(2 - 5/3)/(Sqrt[5] - 5/3), 5],
 N[1/(3 (tauStar - 1)), 5]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"80b70d6e-843b-8446-b5ee-a013cd329db5"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    FractionBox["1", "20"], " ", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"5", "+", 
      RowBox[{"3", " ", 
       SqrtBox["5"]}]}], ")"}]}], ",", "0.5854101966249684545`5.", ",", 
   "0.8848357608504698903`5."}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"6642ddc2-\
b180-464d-b03c-6e73d828058e"],

Cell["\<\
Now the title's converse question: could a classical optical device fake the \
box? On statistics alone the uncomfortable answer is yes, in principle: a \
classical light beam, split and redistributed with tuned intensities, can \
reproduce any consistent table of click frequencies \[LongDash] the \
quantum-optimal one included. That is a theorem, not a defect of imagination, \
and it has a sharp corollary: no function of the observed table alone can \
bound what the device's internal dynamics generate. Breaking the tie takes a \
second, independent lens \[LongDash] audit the dynamics a claimed optical \
design is even capable of generating. Passive linear optics, the \
beamsplitters and phase shifters an intensity forger is limited to, close \
inside the compact algebra u(n), of dimension at most n^2 on n modes; \
squeezing escapes into the full symplectic algebra sp(2n,R), of dimension \
n(2n+1). Which side a design falls on is decidable by an exact-arithmetic \
rank computation, polynomial in the number of modes \[LongDash] in sharp \
contrast to the qubit-side Lie closure, which grows as 4^n - 1 and stalls at \
two glued pentagons.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"aad4cbcb-4438-0943-b90f-b1804e0c8f85"],

Cell["\<\
The audit, self-contained \[LongDash] exact iterated-commutator closure of \
the declared generators in the quadrature representation; staying inside u(n) \
(every element antisymmetric, dimension \[LessEqual] n^2) means the design is \
reproducible by passive linear optics:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"cddde383-3b86-d541-a966-779c35f7bd0e"],

Cell["\<\
comm[a_, b_] := a . b - b . a;
rk[ms_] := If[ms === {}, 0, MatrixRank[Flatten /@ ms]];
indep[ms_] := Fold[If[rk[Append[#1, #2]] > rk[#1], Append[#1, #2], #1] &, {}, \
ms];
closure[gens_] :=
  FixedPoint[indep[Join[#, Flatten[Outer[comm, #, #, 1], 1]]] &, \
indep[gens]];
omega[n_] := KroneckerProduct[IdentityMatrix[n], {{0, 1}, {-1, 0}}];
qf[n_, terms_] := Total[Module[{m = ConstantArray[0, {2 n, 2 n}]},
     m[[#1, #2]] += #3/2; m[[#2, #1]] += #3/2; m] & @@@ terms];
gen[n_, terms_] := omega[n] . qf[n, terms];
phase[n_, j_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 j - 1, 1}, {2 j, 2 j, 1}}];
bsRe[n_, j_, k_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 k - 1, 1}, {2 j, 2 k, 1}}];
bsIm[n_, j_, k_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 k, 1}, {2 j, 2 k - 1, -1}}];
sq1[n_, j_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 j - 1, 1}, {2 j, 2 j, -1}}];
sq2Re[n_, j_, k_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 k - 1, 1}, {2 j, 2 k, -1}}];
sq2Im[n_, j_, k_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 k, 1}, {2 j, 2 k - 1, 1}}];
audit[gens_, n_] := Module[{b = closure[gens]},
   <|\"closure dimension\" -> rk[b], \"u(n) dimension\" -> n^2,
    \"sp(2n,R) dimension\" -> n (2 n + 1),
    \"verdict\" -> If[AllTrue[b, Transpose[#] === -# &] && rk[b] <= n^2,
      \"passive-confined: emulable by linear optics\",
      \"active: genuine squeezing, beyond passive optics\"]|>];
Column[{\"passive interferometer, 2 modes (phases + beamsplitter):\",
  audit[{phase[2, 1], phase[2, 2], bsRe[2, 1, 2], bsIm[2, 1, 2]}, 2],
  \"analogue-Hawking channel (phases + graybody beamsplitter + two-mode \
squeezer):\",
  audit[{phase[2, 1], phase[2, 2], bsRe[2, 1, 2], bsIm[2, 1, 2],
    sq2Re[2, 1, 2], sq2Im[2, 1, 2]}, 2],
  \"single squeezed mode (squeezer + phase), 1 mode:\",
  audit[{phase[1, 1], sq1[1, 1]}, 1]}, Spacings -> 0.8]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"2c910698-002c-4b42-ba0e-54971eafa857"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {"\<\"passive interferometer, 2 modes (phases + beamsplitter):\"\>"},
    {
     RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"\<\"closure dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "4"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"u(n) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "4"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"sp(2n,R) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "10"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"verdict\"\>", 
        "\[Rule]", "\<\"passive-confined: emulable by linear optics\"\>"}]}], 
      "\[RightAssociation]"}]},
    {"\<\"analogue-Hawking channel (phases + graybody beamsplitter + two-mode \
squeezer):\"\>"},
    {
     RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"\<\"closure dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "10"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"u(n) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "4"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"sp(2n,R) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "10"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"verdict\"\>", 
        "\[Rule]", "\<\"active: genuine squeezing, beyond passive \
optics\"\>"}]}], "\[RightAssociation]"}]},
    {"\<\"single squeezed mode (squeezer + phase), 1 mode:\"\>"},
    {
     RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"\<\"closure dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "3"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"u(n) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "1"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"sp(2n,R) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "3"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"verdict\"\>", 
        "\[Rule]", "\<\"active: genuine squeezing, beyond passive \
optics\"\>"}]}], "\[RightAssociation]"}]}
   },
   DefaultBaseStyle->"Column",
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{0.8}}}],
  "Column"]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"59da147f-1551-344b-b40e-1135d3e27813"],

Cell["\<\
Run on three declared designs, the audit returns computed verdicts, not \
promises: a passive two-mode interferometer stays confined at dimension 4 = \
u(2) \[LongDash] emulable; add the two-mode squeezer that models a horizon \
and the closure jumps to dimension 10, all of sp(4,R) \[LongDash] active, \
beyond any passive-optics emulation; even a single squeezed mode already \
escapes (dimension 3 = sp(2,R)). The middle row is the program's flagship \
application under this audit: the analogue-Hawking channel \[LongDash] free \
phase evolution, graybody backscatter as a beamsplitter, pair production at \
the horizon as a two-mode squeezer \[LongDash] is certifiably not \
passive-optics-emulable, even though its correlations, being Gaussian, remain \
efficiently classically simulable. On that channel the two lenses genuinely \
part ways, and both are right \[LongDash] which is precisely why the \
certification battery carries both. Within the class of classical intensity \
emulators, that battery is moreover provably complete \[LongDash] every such \
impostor is either distinguished from a genuine quantum device or certified \
classically bounded \[LongDash] a conditional theorem resting on one stated \
detector-physics premise.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fe6d7216-b951-454b-9d1e-cbd173b9830b"],

Cell["\<\
One verdict is deliberately left unclaimed: the whole-mesh one. Block by \
block, every pentagon of a glued mesh passes the genuine-side audit \
\[LongDash] each block carries the same full rotation algebra as the \
single-pentagon cascade \[LongDash] but a single joint verdict for the \
composed device would take either the qubit-side closure (out of reach beyond \
two pentagons: the two-pentagon case already needs roughly 128 GB of memory, \
three pentagons is terabyte-scale \[LongDash] and, worse, non-discriminating \
exactly here, since direct and twisted rings share identical closure growth \
despite opposite gap behavior) or a physically justified optical compilation \
frame for an arbitrary gluing word, which is new research. The polynomial \
passive-versus-active audit above is the instrument built for that job. \
Pointing it at an arbitrary mesh word is the open frontier on the physics \
side of this essay, standing exactly where the ergodic-optimization \
conjecture stands on its mathematics side.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"997e016a-54fe-3d4e-b2b7-414c4f9f9884"],

Cell["Concluding Remarks", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"b294f226-72cc-d848-80cb-4b3a38a90d79"],

Cell["\<\
Direct and twisted give exact, opposite-fate closed forms for the two \
simplest gluing choices; searching over gluing words instead of just these \
two finds (ddt)\[Infinity], and two facts true of every word \[LongDash] not \
just the ones tried \[LongDash] show it is provably optimal outside a narrow, \
precisely-bounded window, and a very strong candidate everywhere else. A \
single minimal atom of contextuality, composed by nothing more than a choice \
of how to glue its edges together, turns out to hide a genuinely open problem \
in ergodic optimization.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8275dd4c-cf21-414b-a714-1bfc0b37ab4d"],

Cell["\<\
And the composed object is not an end in itself: as the preceding section \
makes concrete, a glued mesh is a black-box certification instrument \
\[LongDash] its gap density the test's statistical power per pentagon, its \
quantum side a single qutrit at a fixed ~88.5% visibility target, and its \
classical-emulation question audited by an exact polynomial \
passive-versus-active criterion, with the whole-mesh verdict standing as the \
physics-side open frontier.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2d74b655-73b7-ff49-93d2-aa69ae0dafc0"],

Cell["The Ceiling and the Floor", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"2c53da89-14ac-c540-a64a-907970230148"],

Cell["\<\
Two facts hold for every gluing word at once, periodic or not, and together \
they close most of the gap the previous section left open. Start with a hard \
ceiling: no matter which word is chosen, \[CurlyTheta]-bar(W) can never \
exceed 3/2. The reason is one accounting trick read two ways: spread weight \
1/2 over every vertex and that alone is already a fractional packing of value \
3/2 per block; spread weight 1/2 instead over each block's two internal edges \
and its two glue-in edges (leaving the shared edge bare) and that's a \
fractional edge cover of the exact same value. Squeezed between a packing and \
a cover of equal size, that value is exact \[LongDash] and since \
\[CurlyTheta] never exceeds a fractional packing number, neither does \
\[CurlyTheta]-bar(W).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"455b8140-a5dc-e947-bdb4-d1d0b995fa18"],

Cell["\<\
A second fact is where ddt's real specialness shows up \[LongDash] not in \
beating twisted, but in what it takes to tie it. For every word, \
\[Alpha]-bar(W) \[GreaterEqual] max(4/3, 1 + f_d/2), where f_d is the \
fraction of joints that are direct. Pure twisted (f_d = 0) reaches the 4/3 \
floor exactly, same as ddt; ddt is singled out only by ALSO maximizing the \
direct-fraction at f_d = 2/3 while still sitting on that floor \[LongDash] no \
other word manages both.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e34e36a7-c36c-3044-a8c5-ddcf07397749"],

Cell["\<\
A scoring trick proves it in three connected steps, all checked live in one \
certificate: score each interface state (0, -1/2, -1); every direct or \
twisted transition then gains at least 3/2 or 1 net of the score change, \
giving density \[GreaterEqual] 1 + f_d/2 in general; the same scoring, run \
three direct letters deep, gives a STRICTLY better rate of 13/3 = 3(4/3) + \
1/3 \[LongDash] so hitting the floor exactly rules out three direct letters \
in a row; and at f_d = 2/3 the direct-runs between twisted letters average \
exactly 2, which, with runs of 3 now forbidden, forces every run to equal \
exactly 2 \[LongDash] precisely the definition of (ddt)^k:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"ecc7a602-49bf-e946-ab6c-88ed16951748"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{phiC = {0, -2^(-1), -1}, phiB = {0, -3^(-1), -2/3}, adjR, Acc, mpMul, \
A3, comb}, adjR[T_, phi_, i_] := Max[Table[T[[i,j]] + phi[[j]] - phi[[i]], \
{j, 3}]]; Acc = Table[dpTransfer[\"d\"][[i,j]] + phiB[[j]] - phiB[[i]], {i, \
3}, {j, 3}]; mpMul[X_, Y_] := Table[Max[Table[X[[i,k]] + Y[[k,j]], {k, 3}]], \
{i, 3}, {j, 3}]; A3 = mpMul[mpMul[Acc, Acc], Acc]; comb = \
AllTrue[Flatten[Table[If[Mod[p, 3] == 0, Module[{ws = Select[Tuples[{\"d\", \
\"t\"}, p], Count[#1, \"d\"] == 2*(p/3) &&  \
!StringContainsQ[StringJoin[StringJoin[#1], StringJoin[#1]], \"ddd\"] & ]}, \
AllTrue[ws, MemberQ[Table[RotateLeft[Characters[StringRepeat[\"ddt\", p/3]], \
r], {r, 0, p - 1}], #1] & ]], True], {p, 3, 12}]], TrueQ]; AllTrue[Range[3], \
adjR[dpTransfer[\"d\"], phiC, #1] >= 3/2 & ] && AllTrue[Range[3], \
adjR[dpTransfer[\"t\"], phiC, #1] >= 1 & ] && Min[Table[Max[A3[[i]]], {i, \
3}]] == 13/3 && comb]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"54dbb0c8-5283-8844-8cd4-7d13abe79e5d"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"78d66e44-b2b1-424c-b6b9-11fd4a8909d3"],

Cell["\<\
Put the ceiling and the floor together and the open question shrinks to a \
narrow strip:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"63372fe3-db70-924c-a1d1-eaf09d505d85"],

Cell["{N[3/2 - (0.0698975), 8], N[0.0714575 - 0.0698975, 6]}", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"453eb5ee-ee8a-d748-96a6-367a65663959"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"1.4301025`", ",", "0.001559999999999992`"}], "}"}]], "Output",Expre\
ssionUUID->"61d2c341-f24b-2042-8b3c-d39a0a5c3912"],

Cell["\<\
The first number is where the ceiling alone already settles things: for \
\[Alpha]-bar(W) \[GreaterEqual] 1.4301025, \[CurlyTheta]-bar(W) \[LessEqual] \
3/2 forces gap(W) \[LessEqual] 3/2 - \[Alpha]-bar(W) \[LessEqual] gap(ddt), \
so ddt cannot be beaten there \[LongDash] proven, not conjectured. The second \
is the certificate-ladder residual from the previous section, bounding every \
OTHER word to within \[Epsilon] \[TildeTilde] 0.0016 of ddt regardless. \
Between the two, ddt's status is about as settled as a genuinely open problem \
can be \[LongDash] only the low-classical-density, ddt-like words near the \
floor are still undecided.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"04cb8a28-7337-e340-af88-c1d71ff97cd1"],

Cell["Future Directions", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"e97d921f-ddf3-dc49-a905-a588d15e3ac8"],

Cell["\<\
Four directions follow directly from the boundary of what is proven above:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"dbfe4a60-12c0-9849-936a-415afcb2b99b"],

Cell["\<\
Close the residual window: ddt's optimality is proven for \[Alpha]-bar(W) \
\[GreaterEqual] 1.4301025 and certified to within \[Epsilon] \[TildeTilde] \
0.0016 elsewhere; the narrow window \[Alpha]-bar(W) \[Element] [4/3, \
1.4301025) remains open and is the sharpest unresolved piece of the argument.\
\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"b07cbea4-d826-8d46-8bd3-97c9b3ace613"],

Cell["\<\
Rule out, or find, an aperiodic competitor: since the finiteness property can \
fail by admitting a non-periodic (Sturmian) maximizer, the natural next step \
is a targeted search or exclusion argument over aperiodic gluing words, not \
further periodic enumeration.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"0c1503d4-f1ab-024f-9b1d-428efe95d04f"],

Cell["\<\
Tighten the certificate ladder: extending \[CapitalGamma]_k to larger window \
sizes k shrinks \[Epsilon] further and narrows the evidence, even though \
\[LongDash] per the obstruction above \[LongDash] it cannot by itself prove \
global optimality.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"1b9193c6-d3e5-9d4a-980b-5a11a0354803"],

Cell["\<\
Point the physical lens at the composition: the polynomial \
passive-versus-active audit of the closing section decides optical \
emulability for declared designs; extending it to a single joint verdict for \
a whole glued mesh needs a physically justified optical compilation frame for \
arbitrary gluing words \[LongDash] the open frontier where this essay's \
mathematics meets its physics.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"fb3347c6-9ae4-c44b-a453-9231b35de398"],

